sop/geometric-normal-sign.evidence:
The backward-difference branch builds tangent (−eps, hCenter−hBack, 0) at lines 60-64, and the same pattern at lines 74-78. The file's own comment (line 50) says tangentU = P(u−eps)−P(u), which is (−eps, hBack−hCenter, 0). Only the all-forward branch is consistent. I ran it in bun on the plane h=0.5x, where the expected normal is (−0.447, 0.894, 0):
- interior vertex (10,·,10): (0.447, 0.894, 0), mirrored in X;
- vertex at x=0: (0.447, −0.894, 0), pointing down;
- vertex at z=0: (−0.447, −0.894, 0), pointing down.
mat/diffuse-shade.ts:57-70 uses these normals for N·L, so Subscapes shading is wrong. The patch's light (−0.5, −0.707, −0.5) at subscapes-terrain-3d.ts:143-148 may have been tuned around the bug.

sop/raymarch-penetration-ao.evidence:
The march steps t += max(dist, 0.5) (line 120) on y − amp·field, which is not a true distance once slopes exceed 45°. Any dist < epsilon, including large negative values, counts as a hit (line 114), with no bisection refinement. The AO loop (lines 73-82) samples height at the same (wx, wz) column as the hit, so for an exact hit sh = h − wy − step is always < 0. In bun:
- on a smooth field, 100 hits all had ao exactly 1;
- on the real Meridian graph at 600×600, 83 of 86 hits were more than epsilon below the surface (worst −34.65 units), and all 61 hits with ao < 1 were among the penetrating ones.

sop/attribute-propagation.evidence:
transform-2d (lines 48-53) and point-orient (lines 73-81) keep everything by spreading `...geometry`. Every other modifier rebuilds the object and loses data:
