One correction on meridian: point-orient does not merely duplicate a scale, it compounds one. It samples field(wx*noiseScale, wz*noiseScale) (point-orient.ts:61) on noiseAngle, which already applies its own noiseScale 0.03 (meridian.ts:49), so the effective frequency is 0.03×0.025.
Why 'partly': the amplitude/baseY duplication is better removed by feeding the SOPs one already-scaled field (see field-algebra-gaps) than by references. Animation is not a need of any current patch, since all are static prints. That makes this medium, not high.

td-fidelity/palette-overloaded.evidence:
Palette is used as:
- a colour set (oklab-palette);
- a single colour constant (subscapes `palette` with count 1, subscapes-terrain-3d.ts:181-189);
- a per-vertex colour array (diffuse-shade returns `{colors, count}` with count = vertexCount, diffuse-shade.ts:131).
Consumers guess:
- stroke-renderer switches to per-vertex when `palette.count === vertexCount` (l.176, 245), otherwise indexes by the depth attribute (l.177, 195), otherwise uses colors[0] (l.75, 115);
- point-renderer cycles `i % palette.count` (point-renderer.ts:75).
A 5-colour palette on 5-vertex geometry is silently read as per-vertex. Per-vertex colour also travels on a separate wire from its geometry (shade and proj both read `cull`, subscapes l.262-269), so any SOP inserted on one branch desynchronises it. In TD per-point colour is the Cd attribute, and colour tables are CHOP channels or a Ramp TOP (my knowledge).

td-fidelity/palette-overloaded.verifyNote:
Palette is used three ways:
- a colour set (oklab-palette);
- a single colour (subscapes palette count 1, subscapes-terrain-3d.ts:181-189);
- a per-vertex array: diffuse-shade returns {colors, count: vertexCount} (diffuse-shade.ts:59, 131).
stroke-renderer infers per-vertex from palette.count === vertexCount (l.176 and l.245), otherwise indexes by depth (l.177, 195-197), otherwise uses colors[0] (l.73-78). point-renderer cycles i % count (l.75). Per-vertex colour reaches the renderer on a separate wire, and shade and proj both read cull (subscapes l.263, 267), so the coupling is by coincidence of vertex count. No current patch hits the count-collision case, but this is a core wire-type ambiguity that the taxonomy refactor has to resolve.

td-fidelity/field-algebra-gaps.evidence:
field-math only combines two fields (field-math.ts:27-30); there is no constant-field source and no field-scale op. As a result:
- height amplitude is a parameter on surface-lift, geometric-normal, base-outline, contour-ring and surface-raymarch;
- domain scale appears separately as noiseScale on the noise ops, `scale` on curl-noise (l.33), noiseScale on point-orient (l.37, 61) and resolution on grid-sampler;
- the FieldExpr union (field-expr.ts:18-71) has no rotation or affine node, so fbm's domainRotation falls back to an opaque closure (fbm.ts:38-49). Meridian uses domainRotation 0.646 (meridian.ts:45), so its whole terrain chain loses its FieldExpr and cannot be GPU-rendered.

td-fidelity/field-algebra-gaps.verifyNote:
Confirmed:
- field-math only combines two fields;
- there is no constant-field operator;
- amplitude lives on surface-lift (l.33), geometric-normal (l.33), base-outline (l.38), contour-ring and surface-raymarch (l.39);
- fbm falls back to an opaque closure when domainRotation ≠ 0 (fbm.ts:38-49), and meridian sets 0.646 (meridian.ts:45).
Refuted part: the algebra is not missing an affine node. FieldExpr already has `apply(fn, ax, ay)` plus arithmetic on X/Y (field-expr.ts:55, 216), and curl-noise already uses apply for scaled coordinates (curl-noise.ts:45-59). fbm's rotation is a single rotate before the octave loop (fbm.ts:59-60), so it is exactly apply(fbmExpr(...), cos·X − sin·Y, sin·X + cos·Y). fbm simply never emits it. A constant is c() (field-expr.ts:187); only an operator is missing.
The consequence is smaller than claimed: meridian has no GPU consumer of that chain (surface-raymarch samples on the CPU), so it does not lose GPU rendering in practice.

td-fidelity/renderer-megaop.evidence:
stroke-renderer has 13 parameters (l.34-55):
- a background colour in bgR/G/B 0-255;
- a fill colour in fillR/G/B;
- a renderMode enum covering stroke, fill and 'oriented dash', the last being per-point instancing from angle/length attributes (l.160-220);
- it also applies the camera transform and reads pixels back.
TD splits this work between a Constant TOP, a Render TOP plus Geometry COMP and MAT, and Composite TOPs; instancing is a Geometry COMP feature (my knowledge). point-renderer repeats the background, camera transform and readback, and no patch uses it. composite sets `layers[i]._canvas = undefined` on its inputs (composite.ts:66,73). That mutates cached upstream outputs, so a later re-cook of composite alone takes the CPU path, whose 'add' blend clamps while the canvas path's 'lighter' does not.

